[Event "Chapter 1: The Big Center Variation / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...Be7 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/a46210b1-d147-419f-9b95-63bed3bd9215/studies/894ab7db-41fc-40fd-a38b-5505c8465645/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. This move is very flexible and can lead to various openings, e.g. King's Indian, Nimzo, Grunfeld, etc. The advantage of our Trompowsky is that we reduce Black's options. 2.Bg5 is the signature move. We may double the pawns, or simply have a pin on the knight after ...e6.} 2.Bg5 2...e6 {Close to 3 million games in the online database go like this. This is 30% of the games recorded. Black wants to defend the knight so that the pawns aren't doubled. 3.e4 is now known as the Big Center Variation. Black cannot take on e4 because their knight is pinned.} 3.e4 3...Be7 {60% of games in the database feature 3...Be7. Black unpins and now threatens e4. We will take even more central space and defend our pawn in the process.} 4.e5 4...Ne4 {4...Ne4 is another square for the knight. Less than 10% of games go like this. The problem for Black is that nothing defends it. First of all, we'll trade.} 5.Bxe7 5...Qxe7 {Black recaptures. Since we traded the dark-squared bishop, we don't mind putting our pawns on dark squares. This is a common strategy you'll see in the Trompowsky. Tactically, we also prevent ...Qb4+.} 6.c3 6...d5 {Black supports their knight. Their big problem is that it has no retreat squares if attacked by our pawns. We'll develop the bishop and start putting some pressure on the knight. We cannot go f3 and h4 too early because Black has ...Qh4+ ideas and we must be careful!} 7.Bd3 7...c5 {Black undermines our center. But our powerful pawn chain holds firm. We can even develop and support it further with the knight. With our development completed, it's almost time to go for f3 and h4.} 8.Ne2 8...Nc6 {The knight is another attacker on d4, but it's sufficiently defended.} 9.f3 {Because our knight is on e2, which lends g3 some protection, we can now push f3. 9...Ng5 gives us the win with 10.h4. And 9...Qh4+ can now be blocked with 10.g3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Big Center Variation / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...Be7 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/a46210b1-d147-419f-9b95-63bed3bd9215/studies/894ab7db-41fc-40fd-a38b-5505c8465645/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. This move is very flexible and can lead to various openings, e.g. King's Indian, Nimzo, Grunfeld, etc. The advantage of our Trompowsky is that we reduce Black's options. 2.Bg5 is the signature move. We may double the pawns, or simply have a pin on the knight after ...e6.} 2.Bg5 2...e6 {Close to 3 million games in the online database go like this. This is 30% of the games recorded. Black wants to defend the knight so that the pawns aren't doubled. 3.e4 is now known as the Big Center Variation. Black cannot take on e4 because their knight is pinned.} 3.e4 3...Be7 {60% of games in the database feature 3...Be7. Black unpins and now threatens e4. We will take even more central space and defend our pawn in the process.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {This invites a trade of bishops. About 90% of games feature this move. The knight on d5 is centralized, but it can be kicked with moves like c4. First, though, we have to trade.} 5.Bxe7 5...Qxe7 {5...Qxe7 is the most popular way to recapture. After all, it makes sense to want to get another piece out of its home square. We'll attack the knight and force it to a worse square.} 6.c4 6...Qb4+ {Black can attack the b2-pawn with check. But as long as we develop and have a tempo on the d5-knight, we don't care about b2.} 7.Nd2 7...Nb6 {The knight moves to b6, but Black's pressure amounts to nothing. With Qc2, we can simply overprotect the c4-pawn and start getting all of our forces mobilized.} 8.Qc2 8...Nc6 {Black attacks d4. But we're able to develop and defend the pawn.} 9.Nf3 9...O-O {Black castles.} 10.Bd3 {Our huge space advantage on both sides of the board is no laughing matter. It's the reason the engine says we have an edge. We can go a3 and b4, but if 10...g6 we can also play 11.h4. Black's king can get into serious trouble!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Big Center Variation / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...Be7 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/a46210b1-d147-419f-9b95-63bed3bd9215/studies/894ab7db-41fc-40fd-a38b-5505c8465645/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. This move is very flexible and can lead to various openings, e.g. King's Indian, Nimzo, Grunfeld, etc. The advantage of our Trompowsky is that we reduce Black's options. 2.Bg5 is the signature move. We may double the pawns, or simply have a pin on the knight after ...e6.} 2.Bg5 2...e6 {Close to 3 million games in the online database go like this. This is 30% of the games recorded. Black wants to defend the knight so that the pawns aren't doubled. 3.e4 is now known as the Big Center Variation. Black cannot take on e4 because their knight is pinned.} 3.e4 3...Be7 {60% of games in the database feature 3...Be7. Black unpins and now threatens e4. We will take even more central space and defend our pawn in the process.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {This invites a trade of bishops. About 90% of games feature this move. The knight on d5 is centralized, but it can be kicked with moves like c4. First, though, we have to trade.} 5.Bxe7 5...Nxe7 {This is also possible and often played. Black figures that c4 is coming, so it's fine to move the knight backwards. However, notice all the kingside space that our d4/e5 pawn duo gives us. After Bd3, our bishop points menacingly at the black kingside.} 6.Bd3 6...d6 {6...d6 is an attempt from Black to undermine our strong pawn chain. We of course will not capture on d6, because we would give up our pawn bridgehead on e5. Instead, we'll develop our queen and attack g7.} 7.Qg4 7...O-O {Black castles. We should just continue to develop.} 8.Nc3 8...dxe5 {This capture isn't a concern. Our d-pawn can take the place of our e5-pawn.} 9.dxe5 9...Nbc6 {Black develops another piece and targets e5.} 10.Nf3 {The engine gives us a +2 position already. The powerful e5-pawn makes any defense of the king extremely difficult. We will castle kingside, play Ng5 and attack that h7-pawn until Black's defenses crumble.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Big Center Variation / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...Be7 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/a46210b1-d147-419f-9b95-63bed3bd9215/studies/894ab7db-41fc-40fd-a38b-5505c8465645/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. This move is very flexible and can lead to various openings, e.g. King's Indian, Nimzo, Grunfeld, etc. The advantage of our Trompowsky is that we reduce Black's options. 2.Bg5 is the signature move. We may double the pawns, or simply have a pin on the knight after ...e6.} 2.Bg5 2...e6 {Close to 3 million games in the online database go like this. This is 30% of the games recorded. Black wants to defend the knight so that the pawns aren't doubled. 3.e4 is now known as the Big Center Variation. Black cannot take on e4 because their knight is pinned.} 3.e4 3...Be7 {60% of games in the database feature 3...Be7. Black unpins and now threatens e4. We will take even more central space and defend our pawn in the process.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {This invites a trade of bishops. About 90% of games feature this move. The knight on d5 is centralized, but it can be kicked with moves like c4. First, though, we have to trade.} 5.Bxe7 5...Qxe7 {5...Qxe7 is the most popular way to recapture. After all, it makes sense to want to get another piece out of its home square. We'll attack the knight and force it to a worse square.} 6.c4 6...Nb6 {The knight on b6 is not so effective. It attacks the c4-pawn, but we are defending it. We can simply develop our bishop to d3, aiming at h7 for when Black castles.} 7.Bd3 7...c5 {7...c5 now is smart if we fall for it: Black wants to undermine our central pawn chain. We won't take on c5. Rather, we'll counter with 8.Qg4 and attack g7.} 8.Qg4 8...O-O {White castles. Recall that the ingredients for the Greek Gift are almost all on the board. We'll complete the configuration by developing our knight to f3.} 9.Nf3 9...cxd4 {Taking on d4 for example loses.} 10.Bxh7+ {The Greek Gift! We are completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Big Center Variation / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...Be7 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/a46210b1-d147-419f-9b95-63bed3bd9215/studies/894ab7db-41fc-40fd-a38b-5505c8465645/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. This move is very flexible and can lead to various openings, e.g. King's Indian, Nimzo, Grunfeld, etc. The advantage of our Trompowsky is that we reduce Black's options. 2.Bg5 is the signature move. We may double the pawns, or simply have a pin on the knight after ...e6.} 2.Bg5 2...e6 {Close to 3 million games in the online database go like this. This is 30% of the games recorded. Black wants to defend the knight so that the pawns aren't doubled. 3.e4 is now known as the Big Center Variation. Black cannot take on e4 because their knight is pinned.} 3.e4 3...Be7 {60% of games in the database feature 3...Be7. Black unpins and now threatens e4. We will take even more central space and defend our pawn in the process.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {This invites a trade of bishops. About 90% of games feature this move. The knight on d5 is centralized, but it can be kicked with moves like c4. First, though, we have to trade.} 5.Bxe7 5...Qxe7 {5...Qxe7 is the most popular way to recapture. After all, it makes sense to want to get another piece out of its home square. We'll attack the knight and force it to a worse square.} 6.c4 6...Nb6 {The knight on b6 is not so effective. It attacks the c4-pawn, but we are defending it. We can simply develop our bishop to d3, aiming at h7 for when Black castles.} 7.Bd3 7...d6 {Black pushes trying to undermine our center. In this case, because Black hasn't committed to castling, we'll take on d6.} 8.exd6 8...cxd6 {Black recaptures. If 8...Qxd6 there's 9.c5. We'll continue developing, and our center is still big. Black has a lot of catching up to do. This is one of the benefits of this variation.} 9.Nf3 9...O-O {Black castles.} 10.O-O {And so do we. We can now expect a promising middlegame thanks to the fact that we have taken the game in a direction that's favorable for us. Re1, Nc3 or Nbd2, Qc2 and Rad1 are some simple yet effective plans.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Big Center Variation / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...h6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/a46210b1-d147-419f-9b95-63bed3bd9215/studies/2029ca0b-6f1f-4a57-bc95-a8f8cdc8efda/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays the flexible 1...Nf6. But we take the game into our own theory with the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...e6 {2...e6 is the most popular move. We go for the Big Center.} 3.e4 3...h6 {Now 3...h6, played roughly 20% of the time, is the subject of this study. Black wants us to either trade, or move the bishop away from the pin. Since moving away is bad (4.Bh4, Bf4, etc.) we'll trade. The results of this trade are that the queen will go to f6, but Black loses an important minor piece that fought for central control.} 4.Bxf6 4...Qxf6 {The queen recaptures, but its pressure will never be the same as the knight's. And we'll simply develop our knight so that both our central pawns are defended.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb4 {5...Bb4 is played almost 45% of the time. It's reasonable because Black doesn't want to be passive. We'll defend our knight and prevent the doubled pawns. 6...0-0 and we can continue with our main plan of 0-0-0 and Nge2.} 6.Qd3 6...Nc6 {This is the most popular move. Black develops and attacks d4. We castle and defend our pawn.} 7.O-O-O 7...Qxf2 {This pawn cannot be taken without consequence. After Nf3, the queen simply isn't getting back out.} 8.Nf3 8...Bd6 {If we play Rd2 now, Bf4 pins our rook to the king.} 9.e5 {But thanks to the fact we have a strong pawn duo, we can push e5 and attack the bishop. The queen is in a lot of trouble and we are winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Big Center Variation / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...h6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/a46210b1-d147-419f-9b95-63bed3bd9215/studies/2029ca0b-6f1f-4a57-bc95-a8f8cdc8efda/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays the flexible 1...Nf6. But we take the game into our own theory with the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...e6 {2...e6 is the most popular move. We go for the Big Center.} 3.e4 3...h6 {Now 3...h6, played roughly 20% of the time, is the subject of this study. Black wants us to either trade, or move the bishop away from the pin. Since moving away is bad (4.Bh4, Bf4, etc.) we'll trade. The results of this trade are that the queen will go to f6, but Black loses an important minor piece that fought for central control.} 4.Bxf6 4...Qxf6 {The queen recaptures, but its pressure will never be the same as the knight's. And we'll simply develop our knight so that both our central pawns are defended.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb4 {5...Bb4 is played almost 45% of the time. It's reasonable because Black doesn't want to be passive. We'll defend our knight and prevent the doubled pawns. 6...0-0 and we can continue with our main plan of 0-0-0 and Nge2.} 6.Qd3 6...Nc6 {This is the most popular move. Black develops and attacks d4. We castle and defend our pawn.} 7.O-O-O 7...O-O {Black castles as well.} 8.g3 {We're going for f4, Nf3 and a kingside attack. Black's lack of space is evident here as well. We are better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Big Center Variation / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...h6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/a46210b1-d147-419f-9b95-63bed3bd9215/studies/2029ca0b-6f1f-4a57-bc95-a8f8cdc8efda/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays the flexible 1...Nf6. But we take the game into our own theory with the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...e6 {2...e6 is the most popular move. We go for the Big Center.} 3.e4 3...h6 {Now 3...h6, played roughly 20% of the time, is the subject of this study. Black wants us to either trade, or move the bishop away from the pin. Since moving away is bad (4.Bh4, Bf4, etc.) we'll trade. The results of this trade are that the queen will go to f6, but Black loses an important minor piece that fought for central control.} 4.Bxf6 4...Qxf6 {The queen recaptures, but its pressure will never be the same as the knight's. And we'll simply develop our knight so that both our central pawns are defended.} 5.Nc3 5...c5 {This move is very natural, and it's played around 10% of the time. The idea is to undermine our big center. Since there were too many pieces converging on our d4-pawn, we'll attack one of them, the queen. It must now go to a worse square.} 6.e5 6...Qd8 {This is the reason why 5...c5 is bad. The queen can be kicked away, and we are left with two dynamic pawns as a result of our first 3 Trompowsky moves. We can even push d5 and threaten d6 in some variations. This may cause Black to worry.} 7.d5 7...d6 {7...d6 is a failed attempt to challenge our pawns. Tactics flow from a better position, but they also flow from a position with more space and central control. There isn't a good way to deal with BB5+.} 8.Bb5+ 8...Bd7 {If the bishop blocks, for instance, we don't have to trade right away because our knight on c3 defends our bishop.} 9.dxe6 {We can play this in-between move and take on f7. Black is lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Big Center Variation / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...h6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/a46210b1-d147-419f-9b95-63bed3bd9215/studies/2029ca0b-6f1f-4a57-bc95-a8f8cdc8efda/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays the flexible 1...Nf6. But we take the game into our own theory with the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...e6 {2...e6 is the most popular move. We go for the Big Center.} 3.e4 3...h6 {Now 3...h6, played roughly 20% of the time, is the subject of this study. Black wants us to either trade, or move the bishop away from the pin. Since moving away is bad (4.Bh4, Bf4, etc.) we'll trade. The results of this trade are that the queen will go to f6, but Black loses an important minor piece that fought for central control.} 4.Bxf6 4...Qxf6 {The queen recaptures, but its pressure will never be the same as the knight's. And we'll simply develop our knight so that both our central pawns are defended.} 5.Nc3 5...d6 {5...d6 makes sense. At surface level, they are stopping the e5-push. But e5 is only one of our ideas. Another plan we have is Qd2, 0-0-0 and a kingside attack.} 6.Qd2 6...g6 {The most common line now includes a fianchetto. We castle.} 7.O-O-O 7...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. However, the space advantage is becoming clear in this line also. After f4, we have three strong central pawns.} 8.f4 8...O-O {To stay alive, Black has to delay castling as long as possible, but the majority of intermediate players choose to castle to follow opening principles.} 9.Nf3 {The engine gives us a big advantage here. Again, all because of our space advantage and our incoming attack on the kingside. We have g4 and h4-h5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Early Kingside Fianchetto / Study 1: Black Plays 2...g6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/ad3afc22-4a8e-48f9-a75d-e86128de67c2/studies/f7ed3325-debd-4f7b-8b7f-8fb76d064f46/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with ...Nf6. And we'll play Bg5, the Trompowsky!} 2.Bg5 2...g6 {Black aims for a fianchetto 15% of the time. Against any d4 openings, going for the ...d6, ...g6 and ...Bg7 approach and playing in King's Indian style seems viable. However, we won't let that happen so easily. Let's capture the knight.} 3.Bxf6 3...exf6 {Black is forced to recapture with the e-pawn. Now that we have exchanged our bishop, we will replace it with pawns on the dark squares to limit the opposing bishop. Thus we will play e3 here. Our plan is simple: we want to fianchetto the light-squared bishop as that will be our power piece. We'll castle and place the knights on e2 and c3, after we play c4 to expand in the center.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Expect to see ...Bg7 here as much as 80% of the time, as it is the most logical move. Black will most likely play ...f5 at some point to unleash the bishop, combined with either ...d6 or ...d5. Since Black hasn't struck in the center yet, we will claim it. We don't have to worry about not developing in time as Black has only got the bishop out.} 5.c4 5...O-O {In fact, after they castle... We can activate the queenside knight first. It's important to take as much control of d5 as possible.} 6.Nc3 6...d6 {A much safer option than going for ...c6 and ...d5 is placing the pawn on d6. Black gives up some central control but continues development. We'll continue with our plan of fianchettoing. Black won't be able to contest the long diagonal.} 7.g3 7...f5 {Expect to see this structure from Black in almost every setup here. They have to play ...f5 to activate the bishop. We'll just continue logically. Our knight is coming to e2 next.} 8.Bg2 8...Nd7 {Black develops as well. They actually want to place the knight on f6, from where it will have a chance to control some central squares, and maybe even land on e4 to block our bishop. We don't have to worry about that for now. We'll simply continue to develop.} 9.Nge2 9...Nf6 {Black maneuvers to f6...} 10.O-O {And we will castle, having basically completed development. This is the most common sequence of moves in the 2...g6 Trompowsky. Here, one of the plans would be placing the queen on d3 and pushing the queenside pawns with a4 and b4 for a healthy expansion.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Early Kingside Fianchetto / Study 1: Black Plays 2...g6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/ad3afc22-4a8e-48f9-a75d-e86128de67c2/studies/f7ed3325-debd-4f7b-8b7f-8fb76d064f46/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with ...Nf6. And we'll play Bg5, the Trompowsky!} 2.Bg5 2...g6 {Black aims for a fianchetto 15% of the time. Against any d4 openings, going for the ...d6, ...g6 and ...Bg7 approach and playing in King's Indian style seems viable. However, we won't let that happen so easily. Let's capture the knight.} 3.Bxf6 3...exf6 {Black is forced to recapture with the e-pawn. Now that we have exchanged our bishop, we will replace it with pawns on the dark squares to limit the opposing bishop. Thus we will play e3 here. Our plan is simple: we want to fianchetto the light-squared bishop as that will be our power piece. We'll castle and place the knights on e2 and c3, after we play c4 to expand in the center.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Expect to see ...Bg7 here as much as 80% of the time, as it is the most logical move. Black will most likely play ...f5 at some point to unleash the bishop, combined with either ...d6 or ...d5. Since Black hasn't struck in the center yet, we will claim it. We don't have to worry about not developing in time as Black has only got the bishop out.} 5.c4 5...O-O {In fact, after they castle... We can activate the queenside knight first. It's important to take as much control of d5 as possible.} 6.Nc3 6...c6 {Black could insist on playing ...d5, but that's actually good for us. We'll look to fianchetto and await the expansion.} 7.g3 7...d5 {The point is to trade once it arrives. And leave Black with an isolated pawn in the center.} 8.cxd5 8...cxd5 {Or an IQP, to be precise. Now that pawn will be a clear target since it already can't advance and no pawn can defend it. And we will start targeting it with the fianchetto.} 9.Bg2 9...Be6 {Black defends d5 with the bishop, as there weren't any other real options. But we won't stop adding attackers to the pawn. We play Nge2. The knight is coming to f4.} 10.Nge2 10...Nc6 {Black has to maneuver to defend here, which will make their position extremely passive. We'll attack the pawn with our three minor pieces.} 11.Nf4 11...Ne7 {Black is in time to defend, but look at their pieces.} 12.Qb3 {And a dagger in the position is definitely Qb3, attacking both the d5-pawn once again and the b7-pawn. Our pressure is so great that we don't even have to rush capturing here. We can just castle first.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Early Kingside Fianchetto / Study 1: Black Plays 2...g6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/ad3afc22-4a8e-48f9-a75d-e86128de67c2/studies/f7ed3325-debd-4f7b-8b7f-8fb76d064f46/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with ...Nf6. And we'll play Bg5, the Trompowsky!} 2.Bg5 2...g6 {Black aims for a fianchetto 15% of the time. Against any d4 openings, going for the ...d6, ...g6 and ...Bg7 approach and playing in King's Indian style seems viable. However, we won't let that happen so easily. Let's capture the knight.} 3.Bxf6 3...exf6 {Black is forced to recapture with the e-pawn. Now that we have exchanged our bishop, we will replace it with pawns on the dark squares to limit the opposing bishop. Thus we will play e3 here. Our plan is simple: we want to fianchetto the light-squared bishop as that will be our power piece. We'll castle and place the knights on e2 and c3, after we play c4 to expand in the center.} 4.e3 4...d5 {Black going for ...d5 as early as move 4 is rare (only 7%), but does affect our setup. Our bishop won't be as strong on g2 now since Black has a pawn on d5. Instead, let's play c4 and challenge the center at once.} 5.c4 5...dxc4 {It's better for Black to capture than play ...c6 and leave themselves with an isolated pawn and a ruined pawn structure. We will recapture with the bishop.} 6.Bxc4 6...Bd6 {Instead of going for the fianchetto, Black activates the bishop to d6 which is great for us, because they don't have a defender inbetween the pawns on the kingside. Now that structure is just looking awkward. We'll continue with simple development.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Black castles... And so will we. There is no need to rush.} 8.O-O 8...c6 {Black establishes some central presence here. They don't want to give us too much space.} 9.Nc3 {Which is fine, as we will make the space ourselves. Having developed the knight to c3, we are in an equal but a very easy to play position. On the queenside, a natural plan is Rb1 to support the b4-advance. We should centralize the f-rook and play the prophylactic h3.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 1: The Raptor Main Line (3...Nxg5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/5f7beadb-e6a0-4c41-9548-5236dc0d7259/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play our Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {And rather than letting us take or defending, they move the knight again and attack us! This occurs in 1.6 million games, or like 17% of the games in the database. Our response of h4 is known as the Raptor Variation. We don't mind if Black trades on g5 because the h-file will become open!} 3.h4 3...Nxg5 {Black now trades. Nearly 40% of games go like this. Black has moved their knight three times in the opening, so we don't mind the trade. We'll recapture and control the f6- and h6-squares. We also have an open h-file for our rook.} 4.hxg5 4...e6 {4...e6 has been played in more than 24,000 games, which is like 25% of the games in the database. We have to defend our pawn and we also develop our knight.} 5.Nf3 5...Be7 {Black aims at our g5-pawn. This is the most popular variation and thus the main line of our study. We will of course defend it, with the queen, also preparing 0-0-0.} 6.Qd2 6...d5 {6...d5 now is normal, establishing a pawn on the center. We'll develop our other knight. We already have more space and better pieces. Black lost a lot of time developing their knight, jumping to e4 and then taking on g5.} 7.Nc3 7...c5 {They may try to undermine d4. But we've got 8.e4 to open up the position! Since we are up in development, we don't mind if things become open.} 8.e4 8...dxe4 {Black trades. We'll recapture. The knight on e4 isn't easily removed. Our pawn on g5 now has an extra defender.} 9.Nxe4 9...cxd4 {Black takes on d4. We can castle now. Black does not have a way of holding on to the pawn.} 10.O-O-O 10...Nc6 {If they defend, we can pin this knight. All of our pieces are active, with clear roles. We cannot say the same for Black.} 11.Bb5 11...Bd7 {If they unpin, they block off the d-file and we can now take on d4.} 12.Nxd4 {Black is passive, and it's hard to suggest a move. Our increased space and piece activity is crushing. 12...Nxd4 13.Qxd4 attacks both d7 and g7.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 1: The Raptor Main Line (3...Nxg5) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/5f7beadb-e6a0-4c41-9548-5236dc0d7259/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play our Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {And rather than letting us take or defending, they move the knight again and attack us! This occurs in 1.6 million games, or like 17% of the games in the database. Our response of h4 is known as the Raptor Variation. We don't mind if Black trades on g5 because the h-file will become open!} 3.h4 3...Nxg5 {Black now trades. Nearly 40% of games go like this. Black has moved their knight three times in the opening, so we don't mind the trade. We'll recapture and control the f6- and h6-squares. We also have an open h-file for our rook.} 4.hxg5 4...g6 {Black may be tempted to fianchetto to stop our g-pawn and fix it as a weakness. However, it isn't truly a weakness. We can defend it and it can be a long-term asset. We'll continue to develop.} 5.Nc3 5...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Their goal is to exert pressure on the dark squares. Our plan remains unaltered. We want to castle queenside, and put pawns on dark squares, neutralizing the fianchettoed bishop.} 6.Qd2 6...d5 {Setups with 6...d5 are common. Black wants to gain some space in the center. We'll castle. The rook on d1 also helps support d4 if the queen moves away.} 7.O-O-O 7...Nc6 {Black attacks the d4-pawn. We are now forced to defend the pawn, but playing e3 fits in with our overall plan.} 8.e3 8...Bf5 {The bishop comes out to f5.} 9.Bd3 {We don't mind trading light-squared bishops because we get rid of Black's bishop pair. A good plan is f4 and Nf3. Black will likely castle queenside in order to escape our h-file pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 1: The Raptor Main Line (3...Nxg5) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/5f7beadb-e6a0-4c41-9548-5236dc0d7259/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play our Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {And rather than letting us take or defending, they move the knight again and attack us! This occurs in 1.6 million games, or like 17% of the games in the database. Our response of h4 is known as the Raptor Variation. We don't mind if Black trades on g5 because the h-file will become open!} 3.h4 3...Nxg5 {Black now trades. Nearly 40% of games go like this. Black has moved their knight three times in the opening, so we don't mind the trade. We'll recapture and control the f6- and h6-squares. We also have an open h-file for our rook.} 4.hxg5 4...g6 {Black may be tempted to fianchetto to stop our g-pawn and fix it as a weakness. However, it isn't truly a weakness. We can defend it and it can be a long-term asset. We'll continue to develop.} 5.Nc3 5...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Their goal is to exert pressure on the dark squares. Our plan remains unaltered. We want to castle queenside, and put pawns on dark squares, neutralizing the fianchettoed bishop.} 6.Qd2 6...c5 {A smart idea from Black is to undermine our d4-pawn. If we make the mistake of taking on c5, we will give their bishop on g7 more squares. That's why we'll simply defend the d4-pawn with e3 and try to stay solid on the dark squares.} 7.e3 7...cxd4 {Black wants trades to open up the position, because they have the bishop pair. We'll simply recapture.} 8.exd4 8...d5 {Black can resume in a normal way with 8...d5. This opens up their other bishop. We can toss in a check with Bb5+ here. We know Black wouldn't want to play Nd7, so they'll likely block with 9...Nc6 (a self-pin), or 9...Bd7.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Bd7 {But we're okay with this block. We are able to trade and now Black does not have a bishop pair advantage.} 10.Bxd7+ 10...Qxd7 {The queen recaptures. We'll castle. The dust starts to settle. It is clear we have a strong asset in the g5-pawn.} 11.O-O-O 11...Nc6 {Black attacks d4.} 12.Nf3 {We simply defend. We have excellent h-file prospects. We want to tuck our king in with Kb1 and then see what Black does. One maneuver is Ne2, then c3, to make sure the d4-pawn is extra secure.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 1: The Raptor Main Line (3...Nxg5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/5f7beadb-e6a0-4c41-9548-5236dc0d7259/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play our Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {And rather than letting us take or defending, they move the knight again and attack us! This occurs in 1.6 million games, or like 17% of the games in the database. Our response of h4 is known as the Raptor Variation. We don't mind if Black trades on g5 because the h-file will become open!} 3.h4 3...Nxg5 {Black now trades. Nearly 40% of games go like this. Black has moved their knight three times in the opening, so we don't mind the trade. We'll recapture and control the f6- and h6-squares. We also have an open h-file for our rook.} 4.hxg5 4...e6 {4...e6 has been played in more than 24,000 games, which is like 25% of the games in the database. We have to defend our pawn and we also develop our knight.} 5.Nf3 5...b6 {About 25% of games go like this. Black wants a light-squared bishop fianchetto. The problem with this is that it doesn't fight for the center as Black needs to. If we simply develop, we'll have a better position with more space.} 6.Nc3 6...Bb7 {The bishop goes to b7. Our plan will remain the same: Qd2 and 0-0-0. We also have to go Qd2 now to protect g5.} 7.Qd2 7...Bb4 {If Black chooses to give up the bishop pair, we'll be happy.} 8.a3 {We actually want to encourage them. 8...Bxc3 9.Qxc3 and Black cannot win a pawn on g5 because 9...Bxf3 10.Qxf3 and we're attacking the rook on a8. We have more space and Black doesn't have a good pawn break without giving up the bishops.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 1: The Raptor Main Line (3...Nxg5) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/5f7beadb-e6a0-4c41-9548-5236dc0d7259/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play our Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {And rather than letting us take or defending, they move the knight again and attack us! This occurs in 1.6 million games, or like 17% of the games in the database. Our response of h4 is known as the Raptor Variation. We don't mind if Black trades on g5 because the h-file will become open!} 3.h4 3...Nxg5 {Black now trades. Nearly 40% of games go like this. Black has moved their knight three times in the opening, so we don't mind the trade. We'll recapture and control the f6- and h6-squares. We also have an open h-file for our rook.} 4.hxg5 4...e6 {4...e6 has been played in more than 24,000 games, which is like 25% of the games in the database. We have to defend our pawn and we also develop our knight.} 5.Nf3 5...d5 {5...d5 is a move we can expect to see. Black wants to stake claim to some central squares. We will simply develop our knight and get ready to play Qd2 to castle.} 6.Nc3 6...c5 {At this point, since we have both our knights out and about, we should just go for the pawn break. We'll play e4, when the central tension benefits us.} 7.e4 7...dxe4 {After this capture, our knight will be active on e4. Our knight is safe there and it will pose Black some problems.} 8.Nxe4 8...cxd4 {Since Black's bishop isn't on e7, we can simply recapture with the queen.} 9.Qxd4 {We have many dynamic ideas. So, after 9...Qxd4 10.Nxd4, we'll have two knights in the center. g6 comes to mind, and Ng5. Black will have a tough time defending.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Ne4, 4...h6, and 5...d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/e4efdf8c-1ac3-43dc-b12c-ed81b9063d57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {The knight goes to e4 and attacks our bishop. Now h4 is the Raptor Variation.} 3.h4 3...h6 {3...h6 is played in like 25% of games, in nearly 50,000 games. The two main follow ups are ...d5 or ...c5. This move makes sense because they make us move our bishop again and they don't give us the open h-file. Our bishop is active on the f4-square, and safe.} 4.Bf4 4...d5 {This is our main line. Almost 50% of games go like this. Black defends the knight and opens up their light-squared bishop. 4...d6 is possible instead. 5.Qd3 is an engine recommendation there. After d5, we'll simply connect our pawns on dark squares, now that our bishop is outside of the pawn chain.} 5.e3 5...c5 {5...c5 is a good move. They want to undermine our center. We can increase the pressure by challenging Black's knight on e4. Our d4-pawn is sufficiently defended and there is no need to take on c5.} 6.Nd2 6...Nc6 {6...Nxd2 7.Qxd2 would be a normal game in which we'd castle and play moves like Nf3, continuing the game. But if Black allows us to take on e4, they can get into trouble quickly.} 7.Nxe4 7...dxe4 {Black takes, giving up control of the d5-square. And we'll push forward with the pawn! Black is in some trouble.} 8.d5 8...Nb4 {This is a huge mistake! With c3, we set a very interesting long-range trap. } 9.c3 9...Nxd5 {Black takes, and they may think everything is alright. Even after our check with Bb5, they will simply block with the bishop which in turn attacks our bishop. We don't have time to win a piece.} 10.Bb5+ 10...Bd7 {Their knight is under attack, but so is our bishop! But we can take the knight because Black's position will turn out to be loose.} 11.Qxd5 11...Bxb5 {Black takes the piece.} 12.Qxb7 {Black is lacking in coordination and there is a question of where they will put the bishop. If 12...Bd7, to prevent issue with Qc6+, there's 13.0-0-0, and the bishop is pinned. On top of this, the e-pawn is loose. We are much better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Ne4, 4...h6, and 5...d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/e4efdf8c-1ac3-43dc-b12c-ed81b9063d57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {The knight goes to e4 and attacks our bishop. Now h4 is the Raptor Variation.} 3.h4 3...h6 {3...h6 is played in like 25% of games, in nearly 50,000 games. The two main follow ups are ...d5 or ...c5. This move makes sense because they make us move our bishop again and they don't give us the open h-file. Our bishop is active on the f4-square, and safe.} 4.Bf4 4...d5 {This is our main line. Almost 50% of games go like this. Black defends the knight and opens up their light-squared bishop. 4...d6 is possible instead. 5.Qd3 is an engine recommendation there. After d5, we'll simply connect our pawns on dark squares, now that our bishop is outside of the pawn chain.} 5.e3 5...e6 {5...e6 and 5...Bf5 are basically tied in popularity at 25%. We'll challenge the knight. It's a strong piece on e4.} 6.Nd2 6...Nxd2 {Chances are they'll trade on d2. We'll simply recapture and get ready to castle.} 7.Qxd2 7...c5 {7...c5 is understandable, once again, undermining the center. We'll develop and add a defender.} 8.Nf3 8...Nc6 {If Black adds an attacker...} 9.Ne5 {We'll propose a trade! We've got some neat attacking plans here, but they all stem from 0-0-0 and Be2 to get our pieces going. The bishop on e2 will help the pawns in their path up the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Ne4, 4...h6, and 5...d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/e4efdf8c-1ac3-43dc-b12c-ed81b9063d57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {The knight goes to e4 and attacks our bishop. Now h4 is the Raptor Variation.} 3.h4 3...h6 {3...h6 is played in like 25% of games, in nearly 50,000 games. The two main follow ups are ...d5 or ...c5. This move makes sense because they make us move our bishop again and they don't give us the open h-file. Our bishop is active on the f4-square, and safe.} 4.Bf4 4...d5 {This is our main line. Almost 50% of games go like this. Black defends the knight and opens up their light-squared bishop. 4...d6 is possible instead. 5.Qd3 is an engine recommendation there. After d5, we'll simply connect our pawns on dark squares, now that our bishop is outside of the pawn chain.} 5.e3 5...Bf5 {Black supports their knight and develops a piece. The bad part of this is that we get a massive attack with some simple moves. We can be very primitive in our approach, and just attack the minor pieces with our pawns.} 6.f3 6...Nf6 {The knight goes back to f6. And now we'll attack the bishop. We have a lot of kingside space.} 7.g4 7...Bh7 {Most players go to h7. It's mostly a way to cope, as they pretend they have an active bishop. The reality is that our pawns give us extra space and control the middlegame. There isn't a good way to counter them. We can even develop normally with Nc3.} 8.Nc3 8...e6 {Black now defends d5. As before, we'll play Qd2 and prepare to castle queenside.} 9.Qd2 9...Bd6 {Black proposes a trade of bishops.} 10.O-O-O {But we won't help them develop: instead we'll castle. Even though our pawns are all pushed forward, Black doesn't have a way to attack and win them, nor to infiltrate our position. We don't mind the trade on f4, because we will soon play g5 and attack the knight. We are slightly better.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Ne4, 4...h6, and 5...d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/e4efdf8c-1ac3-43dc-b12c-ed81b9063d57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {The knight goes to e4 and attacks our bishop. Now h4 is the Raptor Variation.} 3.h4 3...h6 {3...h6 is played in like 25% of games, in nearly 50,000 games. The two main follow ups are ...d5 or ...c5. This move makes sense because they make us move our bishop again and they don't give us the open h-file. Our bishop is active on the f4-square, and safe.} 4.Bf4 4...d5 {This is our main line. Almost 50% of games go like this. Black defends the knight and opens up their light-squared bishop. 4...d6 is possible instead. 5.Qd3 is an engine recommendation there. After d5, we'll simply connect our pawns on dark squares, now that our bishop is outside of the pawn chain.} 5.e3 5...e6 {5...e6 and 5...Bf5 are basically tied in popularity at 25%. We'll challenge the knight. It's a strong piece on e4.} 6.Nd2 6...Nxd2 {Chances are they'll trade on d2. We'll simply recapture and get ready to castle.} 7.Qxd2 7...Bd6 {Black can propose a trade here. We'll simply castle.} 8.O-O-O 8...Bxf4 {We don't mind the trade. It in fact benefits us because we recapture and get another strong pawn on the kingside.} 9.exf4 9...O-O {If Black castles, they've walked into our attack.} 10.g4 {We can even play h5 before g5 so that the h6-pawn is fixed in its place and we guarantee the opening of the g-file towards the king. This is a difficult position for Black and we should have a massive attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Ne4, 4...h6, and 5...d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/e4efdf8c-1ac3-43dc-b12c-ed81b9063d57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {The knight goes to e4 and attacks our bishop. Now h4 is the Raptor Variation.} 3.h4 3...h6 {3...h6 is played in like 25% of games, in nearly 50,000 games. The two main follow ups are ...d5 or ...c5. This move makes sense because they make us move our bishop again and they don't give us the open h-file. Our bishop is active on the f4-square, and safe.} 4.Bf4 4...d5 {This is our main line. Almost 50% of games go like this. Black defends the knight and opens up their light-squared bishop. 4...d6 is possible instead. 5.Qd3 is an engine recommendation there. After d5, we'll simply connect our pawns on dark squares, now that our bishop is outside of the pawn chain.} 5.e3 5...e6 {5...e6 and 5...Bf5 are basically tied in popularity at 25%. We'll challenge the knight. It's a strong piece on e4.} 6.Nd2 6...Bd6 {Other moves like 6...Bd6 aren't challenging. Black proposes a trade, which we don't mind. Firstly, we'll take on e4. Black will have two things to worry about: the defense of e4 and the defense of g7.} 7.Nxe4 7...Bxf4 {If this trade happens, we will play the immediate Qg4. We hit both f4 and g7.} 8.Qg4 8...dxe4 {Black takes on e4.} 9.Qxg7 {But we have a tactical solution: an intermezzo! Black has to move the rook, after which we'll recapture the bishop. After breaking the kingside apart, we have a huge advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Ne4, 4...h6, and 5...c5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/8df9ad23-f4f7-477d-bad9-7f9286705e36/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {The knight is developed. We'll play the Trompowsky!} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {Black attacks our bishop. We'll defend it with the h-pawn! The Raptor Variation!} 3.h4 3...h6 {Black refuses to take. The f4-square is best for the dark-squared bishop. We control e5 and leave our bishop actively posted.} 4.Bf4 4...c5 {Black undermines d4. About 15% of games go like this. They clearly want us to take on c5 so we lose control of the center. But instead, we'll push d5, giving us space and influence over Black's position.} 5.d5 5...d6 {This move is played about 45% of the time. Black simply may want to stop our d-pawn's advance, or they may simply want to develop. A great idea in this variation is often Nd2. We undermine the strong knight and tempt Black to trade, allowing Qxd2 developing our queen.} 6.Nd2 6...Nxd2 {Most players just snap and trade. I don't blame them: 6...Bf5 runs into 7.g4 and things get awkward for Black. We'll just recapture. We have two developed pieces, a strong d5-pawn that prevents ...Nc6, and we're about to castle.} 7.Qxd2 7...g6 {Black will fianchetto because their bishop is trapped behind the e7-d6-c5 pawn chain. Our lone-wolf d5-pawn can get a companion after e4.} 8.e4 8...Bg7 {Black gets some dark-square pressure. But it's short-lived, as we can close the diagonal down with c3.} 9.c3 9...Nd7 {The knight may consider e5 as a potential outpost.} 10.Bd3 {We'll just play 10.Bd3. 10...Ne5 11.Bxe5 Bxe5 12.f4 Bg7 is a great position for us, especially if we go for something like 13.h5. Black won't feel comfortable in that variation.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Ne4, 4...h6, and 5...c5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/8df9ad23-f4f7-477d-bad9-7f9286705e36/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {The knight is developed. We'll play the Trompowsky!} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {Black attacks our bishop. We'll defend it with the h-pawn! The Raptor Variation!} 3.h4 3...h6 {Black refuses to take. The f4-square is best for the dark-squared bishop. We control e5 and leave our bishop actively posted.} 4.Bf4 4...c5 {Black undermines d4. About 15% of games go like this. They clearly want us to take on c5 so we lose control of the center. But instead, we'll push d5, giving us space and influence over Black's position.} 5.d5 5...Qb6 {Black gets some counterplay with the queen. 30% of games go like this. Instead, a few players go for 5...e6, but we would just take on e6 and trade queens. We'll play Nd2. Our approach of trading the knights works because we have a solid way of getting counterplay despite the loss of a pawn.} 6.Nd2 6...Nxd2 {Black can begin their sequence by taking on d2. We'll recapture. If Black doesn't take on b2, then we'll play Bc3 next, and their queen will look offside.} 7.Bxd2 7...Qxb2 {Black takes in order to prevent Bc3. We can develop our knight. That's two minor pieces out to Black's none!} 8.Nf3 8...d6 {Black can start making some efforts to develop. But we're activating all our forces at light speed. e4 prepares to develop the f1-bishop.} 9.e4 9...g6 {They have to play the slow 9...g6 to develop the bishop. What else?} 10.Rb1 {But Black's setup is so bad that they'll have to play 10...Qf6 to, again, prevent Bc3 with a tempo on the rook on h8. The b7-pawn is vulnerable, and we have many interesting attacking ideas with Bb5+.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Ne4, 4...h6, and 5...c5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/8df9ad23-f4f7-477d-bad9-7f9286705e36/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {The knight is developed. We'll play the Trompowsky!} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {Black attacks our bishop. We'll defend it with the h-pawn! The Raptor Variation!} 3.h4 3...h6 {Black refuses to take. The f4-square is best for the dark-squared bishop. We control e5 and leave our bishop actively posted.} 4.Bf4 4...c5 {Black undermines d4. About 15% of games go like this. They clearly want us to take on c5 so we lose control of the center. But instead, we'll push d5, giving us space and influence over Black's position.} 5.d5 5...Qb6 {Black gets some counterplay with the queen. 30% of games go like this. Instead, a few players go for 5...e6, but we would just take on e6 and trade queens. We'll play Nd2. Our approach of trading the knights works because we have a solid way of getting counterplay despite the loss of a pawn.} 6.Nd2 6...Qxb2 {If the queen takes right away, we can take the knight on e4. While we aren't winning a piece, we'll get a lot of time for development.} 7.Nxe4 7...Qb4+ {Black gives a check in order to regain the piece. 8.c3 is a useful way to block the check because we don't want to play Bd2 and worsen our bishop.} 8.c3 8...Qxe4 {Black recaptures their piece, which is fair.} 9.e3 {But look at us now! Our f1-bishop is ready to come out, and so is the knight on f3. We have Nf3, but if 9...d6 we can even go for something more aggressive like 10.f3 followed by Bd3. We should have no problems having a good middlegame. The lost b2-pawn won't be relevant as we have such good compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Raptor Variation / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Ne4, 4...h6, and 5...c5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/d0ca1b39-19c9-4212-a239-10dd1330602c/studies/8df9ad23-f4f7-477d-bad9-7f9286705e36/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {The knight is developed. We'll play the Trompowsky!} 2.Bg5 2...Ne4 {Black attacks our bishop. We'll defend it with the h-pawn! The Raptor Variation!} 3.h4 3...h6 {Black refuses to take. The f4-square is best for the dark-squared bishop. We control e5 and leave our bishop actively posted.} 4.Bf4 4...c5 {Black undermines d4. About 15% of games go like this. They clearly want us to take on c5 so we lose control of the center. But instead, we'll push d5, giving us space and influence over Black's position.} 5.d5 5...d6 {This move is played about 45% of the time. Black simply may want to stop our d-pawn's advance, or they may simply want to develop. A great idea in this variation is often Nd2. We undermine the strong knight and tempt Black to trade, allowing Qxd2 developing our queen.} 6.Nd2 6...Qa5 {This pin may help Black feel like they're putting some pressure on us. But, in reality, if they'll help us play Bd2 and Bc3, we're feeling happy about this situation.} 7.f3 7...Nxd2 {The knight takes on d2. We recapture. This is a tempo on the queen, so we're doing our maneuver with gain of time.} 8.Bxd2 8...Qb6 {The queen goes to b6, attacking b2.} 9.Bc3 {But in this variation, we get our bishop to c3 quickly. Black has wasted some time and all they have to show for it is a queen on b6. We will play Qd2 and e4, with more space and with a far better coordinated piece formation.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...exf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/0f560129-df45-472d-8dd2-7d51b4d66bbd/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays 1...Nf6. And we'll play our Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {2...d5 occurs in 15% of games. Black doesn't care about the doubled pawns and in fact invites them. We'll trade and set our pawns up on dark squares.} 3.Bxf6 3...exf6 {More than 70% of games feature 3...exf6, which is the subject of our study. This move opens up the dark-squared bishop. We will now place our pawns on dark squares, beginning with e3, and with the option of playing c4 in the future. Black has many options here, but very few of them actually stop our simple plan of c4, Nc3.} 4.e3 4...c5 {4...c5 challenges our center right away. This is played almost 10% of the time. Let's stay solid here. We'll simply defend d4 and develop a piece.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {The knight increases pressure on d4. We have enough defenders, so we should just develop. We'll play Be2.} 6.Be2 6...cxd4 {Should Black take here, we should trade knights, or at least offer a trade. Thus we'll recapture with the knight.} 7.Nxd4 7...Qb6 {7...Qb6 attacks our b2-pawn. Nxd4 would just have brought our queen into the game. But the attacked b-pawn really isn't a concern. We have some tactics with the knights. We can attack d5 with Nc3. If Black takes on b2, we take on d5. As simple as that.} 8.Nc3 8...Bb4 {The pin is one of the ways Black has of defending the d-pawn.} 9.Qd3 {Black's 7...Qb6 wasn't anything special. We don't mind a trade on d4, since we can just recapture.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...exf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/0f560129-df45-472d-8dd2-7d51b4d66bbd/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays 1...Nf6. And we'll play our Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {2...d5 occurs in 15% of games. Black doesn't care about the doubled pawns and in fact invites them. We'll trade and set our pawns up on dark squares.} 3.Bxf6 3...exf6 {More than 70% of games feature 3...exf6, which is the subject of our study. This move opens up the dark-squared bishop. We will now place our pawns on dark squares, beginning with e3, and with the option of playing c4 in the future. Black has many options here, but very few of them actually stop our simple plan of c4, Nc3.} 4.e3 4...c5 {4...c5 challenges our center right away. This is played almost 10% of the time. Let's stay solid here. We'll simply defend d4 and develop a piece.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {The knight increases pressure on d4. We have enough defenders, so we should just develop. We'll play Be2.} 6.Be2 6...Qb6 {Black can attack our b2-pawn. But we can attack their d-pawn as well with Nc3. Alternatively, natural moves like 6...Bd6 and 6...Be6 pose no problems. Against both of them we can respond with 7.0-0 and then consider opening up the position.} 7.Nc3 7...Be6 {The bishop defends d5. We'll get our king to safety.} 8.O-O 8...Qxb2 {If Black takes on b2, they lose defense of c7.} 9.Nb5 {We are threatening Nc7+ and Rb1. Black will have a tough time defending, and a worse position. We can toy with the queen by attacking it with our rooks, and also connect our rooks with Qd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...exf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/0f560129-df45-472d-8dd2-7d51b4d66bbd/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays 1...Nf6. And we'll play our Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {2...d5 occurs in 15% of games. Black doesn't care about the doubled pawns and in fact invites them. We'll trade and set our pawns up on dark squares.} 3.Bxf6 3...exf6 {More than 70% of games feature 3...exf6, which is the subject of our study. This move opens up the dark-squared bishop. We will now place our pawns on dark squares, beginning with e3, and with the option of playing c4 in the future. Black has many options here, but very few of them actually stop our simple plan of c4, Nc3.} 4.e3 4...Be6 {This is a rare move, but it has independent significance and is worthy of showing. The point is Black stops c4. Instead, 5.Nd2 once again prepares c4.} 5.Nd2 5...Bd6 {Black develops both bishops harmoniously. We've got 6.c4 at this point. ...dxc4 only helps us.} 6.c4 6...c6 {Black defends the center. We won't take on d5 because we don't have Nc3 to add pressure. So instead we'll develop our knight to f3. Black has played the opening well, so our goal now is to simply set up in the best way possible.} 7.Ngf3 7...O-O {Black castles. Castling kingside is safe for them because we haven't gone for any expansion on that side of the board. We'll develop our bishop and point it at the black kingside.} 8.Bd3 8...Nd7 {Black develops their knight. d7 is definitely the right path for it. Now we'll set up a battery, with our queen and bishop attacking h7.} 9.Qc2 9...g6 {Black defends this without problems.} 10.O-O {And we castle. Our plan now involves queenside play with c5, b4, a4 and b5. Black wants to play f5, Nf6 and Ne4. We have a very rich position with play for both sides.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...exf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/0f560129-df45-472d-8dd2-7d51b4d66bbd/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays 1...Nf6. And we'll play our Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {2...d5 occurs in 15% of games. Black doesn't care about the doubled pawns and in fact invites them. We'll trade and set our pawns up on dark squares.} 3.Bxf6 3...exf6 {More than 70% of games feature 3...exf6, which is the subject of our study. This move opens up the dark-squared bishop. We will now place our pawns on dark squares, beginning with e3, and with the option of playing c4 in the future. Black has many options here, but very few of them actually stop our simple plan of c4, Nc3.} 4.e3 4...Bd6 {Bringing the bishop out to d6 is the most popular line. 4...c6 was also possible, but it leads to the same line in most cases. We'll play 5.c4 to undermine the d-pawn. Our main plan in this variation is to apply pressure with c4, cxd5 and Nc3.} 5.c4 5...c6 {Black defends d5. This is why I had said that 4...c6 often leads to this position as well. We take. This is good for us because Black gets an isolated pawn on the d-file. Coupled with the fact that they have doubled f-pawns, their structure isn't looking healthy.} 6.cxd5 6...cxd5 {Black recaptures. We can now develop and increase pressure on d5.} 7.Nc3 7...Be6 {Black defends. While we aren't going to win the pawn immediately, we'll be able to exert pressure at any stage of the game. 8.g3 comes with the idea that we'll keep an eye on d5.} 8.g3 8...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto. Our setup is nearly complete.} 9.Bg2 9...Bb4 {Most players here go 9...Bb4 to pin the attacker. The bishop moving away also opens up the defense of the queen on d5.} 10.Ne2 {We of course won't let Black remove one of our active attackers. If 10...Bxc3, then 11.Nxc3. In general terms, we will castle, play Rc1, and be content that we have long-term pressure on d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...gxf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/82e09901-3734-4f5f-a984-de7f172dab73/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll go for the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {Black focuses on gaining space in the center. We'll take the knight to double the pawns.} 3.Bxf6 3...gxf6 {Roughly 25% of the games in the database go like this. According to the engine, both recaptures are fine, so it's up to us to see what the main differences are. Black gets an open g-file, should they want to use it. We will place our pawns on dark squares like always.} 4.e3 4...e5 {More than 30% of games continue like this. Black goes for this direct pawn break, which is a benefit of having recaptured 3...gxf6. They still have an e-pawn. We should just develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 5.Nf3 5...Bg4 {Black could pin us, thinking that e4 will pose some practical problems. This is also a way of defending e5. But we will calmly unpin with Be2.} 6.Be2 6...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We can now build this solid pawn formation on the dark squares. Our queen also gains access to more squares after our move c3.} 7.c3 7...Qd7 {The queen comes out to d7. Since the kingside is open, Black will likely go for ...0-0-0. We should develop our other knight. Since our pawn is on c3, we go to d2.} 8.Nbd2 8...O-O-O {Black castles. Against this, we also have an aggressive plan.} 9.b4 {We have ideas of b5 and Qa5 to attack a7. Here's another thing to note: because of the open g-file, we may want to delay castling as much as we can. In fact, we have a strong center in with the pawns on c3, d4 and e3. Leaving the king in the center for a few moves won't hurt, and we'll be able to expand on the queenside while we see if Black wants to use the g-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...gxf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/82e09901-3734-4f5f-a984-de7f172dab73/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll go for the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {Black focuses on gaining space in the center. We'll take the knight to double the pawns.} 3.Bxf6 3...gxf6 {Roughly 25% of the games in the database go like this. According to the engine, both recaptures are fine, so it's up to us to see what the main differences are. Black gets an open g-file, should they want to use it. We will place our pawns on dark squares like always.} 4.e3 4...e5 {More than 30% of games continue like this. Black goes for this direct pawn break, which is a benefit of having recaptured 3...gxf6. They still have an e-pawn. We should just develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {The knight is developed to defend e5. We'll go for our solid dark square pawn formation.} 6.c3 6...Bg4 {If the bishop pins us, Be2 isn't necessary because we already played c3, supporting d4.} 7.Qa4 {Our queen can move away to a useful square. We have promising ideas of Bb5, or dxe5 to discover an attack on the g4-bishop. A funny mistake could be 7...Bd6 8.dxe5 and both Black's bishops are under attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...gxf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/82e09901-3734-4f5f-a984-de7f172dab73/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll go for the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {Black focuses on gaining space in the center. We'll take the knight to double the pawns.} 3.Bxf6 3...gxf6 {Roughly 25% of the games in the database go like this. According to the engine, both recaptures are fine, so it's up to us to see what the main differences are. Black gets an open g-file, should they want to use it. We will place our pawns on dark squares like always.} 4.e3 4...e5 {More than 30% of games continue like this. Black goes for this direct pawn break, which is a benefit of having recaptured 3...gxf6. They still have an e-pawn. We should just develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 5.Nf3 5...e4 {5...e4 isn't a challenge for us. Our knight is safe on d2 and it will help us pushing c4, undermining the pawns on the light squares. The popular 6...Nc6 only blocks the c-pawn in and we can simply answer with 7.c4.} 6.Nfd2 6...c5 {Black may want to counter our center first. Since we have a knight on d2, we can take the c-pawn and then move our knight with tempo on the bishop.} 7.dxc5 7...Bxc5 {The bishop recaptures, but here it can be attacked. Our knight also helps us control the outpost on d4. It seems this entire 6...c5 operation from Black is a total failure.} 8.Nb3 8...Bb6 {The bishop retreats to b6, where it keeps an eye on d4. This is a very normal-looking move, but it leads to good play for us. We can develop and attack d5.} 9.Nc3 9...Be6 {Black's bishop defends.} 10.Qh5 {Black's huge issue is that they made themselves a weakness on d5. We will castle and win that pawn easily. We are winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...gxf6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/82e09901-3734-4f5f-a984-de7f172dab73/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll go for the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {Black focuses on gaining space in the center. We'll take the knight to double the pawns.} 3.Bxf6 3...gxf6 {Roughly 25% of the games in the database go like this. According to the engine, both recaptures are fine, so it's up to us to see what the main differences are. Black gets an open g-file, should they want to use it. We will place our pawns on dark squares like always.} 4.e3 4...Bf5 {This occurs in 15% of games. The bishop comes out now so that e6 doesn't restrict it. We'll undermine the center with c4.} 5.c4 5...c6 {Black defends d5. We'll develop with Nc3, attacking the d5-pawn and increasing the pressure.} 6.Nc3 6...e6 {Here it's nice to see one of the main differences in the ...gxf6 variation. Black still has an e-pawn that they can defend d5 with.} 7.Bd3 {If Black trades on d3, our queen is developed and we are happy. If they go to g6, we will simply continue with normal development like Nf3, and we could even push the h-pawn to attack the bishop. We can also insert the cxd5 capture so that our bishop isn't distracted with having to recapture on c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...gxf6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/82e09901-3734-4f5f-a984-de7f172dab73/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll go for the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {Black focuses on gaining space in the center. We'll take the knight to double the pawns.} 3.Bxf6 3...gxf6 {Roughly 25% of the games in the database go like this. According to the engine, both recaptures are fine, so it's up to us to see what the main differences are. Black gets an open g-file, should they want to use it. We will place our pawns on dark squares like always.} 4.e3 4...Bf5 {This occurs in 15% of games. The bishop comes out now so that e6 doesn't restrict it. We'll undermine the center with c4.} 5.c4 5...e6 {Black defends d5 with 5...e6. We should now take on d5.} 6.cxd5 6...Qxd5 {The queen recaptures, as 6...exd5 leaves Black with a horrible pawn structure. But on d5 we can attack it and develop with tempo!} 7.Nc3 7...Bb4 {The bishop pins the knight.} 8.Ne2 {But our idea is to recapture on c3 with our other knight and attack the queen once again. We have a plan of a3 and b4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...d5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...gxf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/5432ad86-ebc4-4578-8c73-fb736c7789f3/studies/82e09901-3734-4f5f-a984-de7f172dab73/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll go for the Trompowsky.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {Black focuses on gaining space in the center. We'll take the knight to double the pawns.} 3.Bxf6 3...gxf6 {Roughly 25% of the games in the database go like this. According to the engine, both recaptures are fine, so it's up to us to see what the main differences are. Black gets an open g-file, should they want to use it. We will place our pawns on dark squares like always.} 4.e3 4...e5 {More than 30% of games continue like this. Black goes for this direct pawn break, which is a benefit of having recaptured 3...gxf6. They still have an e-pawn. We should just develop our knight and attack the pawn.} 5.Nf3 5...e4 {5...e4 isn't a challenge for us. Our knight is safe on d2 and it will help us pushing c4, undermining the pawns on the light squares. The popular 6...Nc6 only blocks the c-pawn in and we can simply answer with 7.c4.} 6.Nfd2 6...f5 {It's natural for Black to make use of this pawn. They get a solid pawn chain. But our plan of 7.c4 isn't stopped. We'll play it, and undermine the d5-pawn.} 7.c4 7...c6 {Black defends d5. We are happy to capture on d5, fixing the weakness in place.} 8.cxd5 8...cxd5 {Black recaptures.} 9.Nc3 {We will have a lot of play against the d5-pawn. We want to play Be2 and develop, possibly castling queenside, but there is no immediate rush since we'd like to see what Black will do.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...h6 {2...h6 is very popular at the amateur level: the bishop is such a nuisance that Black wants to remove it immediately. I suggest we just stay on the diagonal that perturbs Black the most.} 3.Bh4 3...g5 {Tactically speaking, our bishop won't get trapped. I suggest we retreat to g3.} 4.Bg3 4...Nf6 {Nf6 covers the h5 square. But now we can just cover the f4 square.} 5.e3 5...d6 {Black sets up counterplay with future pawn breaks. But we'll play h4 and achieve a pawn break of our own!} 6.h4 6...Rg8 {Moving the rook off the h-file is another option. In which case I suggest trading on g5...} 7.hxg5 7...hxg5 {Black recaptures. We won't be castling kingside, since our rook is happy on the h-file. So let's develop the queenside pieces.} 8.Nc3 8...e6 {Black supports f5. We'll prepare 0-0-0.} 9.Qd2 9...Qe7 {The queen is developed. Maybe e5 is an idea. Though because Black is so down in development, they shouldn't open up the position.} 10.O-O-O {Now we can castle. If the position opens up, our pieces will come to life.} 10...Nc6 {Simple developing moves from Black can be played...} 11.Bc4 {But our pieces are just better, and it's hard to suggest Black a way to make progress. White enjoys a stable edge.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...g6 {The move most masters play is 2...g6. Let's just develop aggressively with Nc3!} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {The bishop goes to g7. And here comes our h-pawn racing forward!} 4.h4 4...Nf6 {4...Nf6 allows us to destroy the kingside: We push forward! The pawn structure will be changed one way or the other.} 5.h5 5...Nxh5 {If the knight takes... We'll have enough compensation for this exchange.} 6.Rxh5 6...gxh5 {Black recaptures and the pawns are wrecked. We can attack e7 with Nd5.} 7.Nd5 7...Nc6 {Black defends.} 8.e3 {And White is better. Getting compensation was easy money.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...h6 {2...h6 is very popular at the amateur level: the bishop is such a nuisance that Black wants to remove it immediately. I suggest we just stay on the diagonal that perturbs Black the most.} 3.Bh4 3...g5 {Tactically speaking, our bishop won't get trapped. I suggest we retreat to g3.} 4.Bg3 4...Nf6 {Nf6 covers the h5 square. But now we can just cover the f4 square.} 5.e3 5...d6 {Black sets up counterplay with future pawn breaks. But we'll play h4 and achieve a pawn break of our own!} 6.h4 6...g4 {If 6...g4... h5 prevents Black from playing h5 themselves, supporting g4, and it gives the h4 square back to the bishop.} 7.h5 7...Be6 {The bishop is developed... Our knight can come to f4, defending h5.} 8.Ne2 8...Bf7 {So once the bishop goes to f7... The knight arrives on f4, defending the pawn.} 9.Nf4 9...e5 {9...e5 doesn't work because... We can just take.} 10.dxe5 10...dxe5 {Black recaptures. We'll exchange queens and disrupt the black king's position.} 11.Qxd8+ 11...Kxd8 {They lose castling rights when they recapture.} 12.Ng6 {And we unleash an attack on e5 and the rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...c6 {Black may have ideas of pawn hunting. We can just develop normally.} 3.e3 3...Qb6 {If Black thinks they can just grab material and not give us activity, they have another think coming. We can just ignore the threat and play Nd2.} 4.Nd2 4...Qxb2 {It isn't a threat at all if the queen's position can be exploited. We'll bring our rook to the semi-open b-file, attacking the black queen.} 5.Rb1 5...Qa3 {The queen gets out of the way.} 6.Bd3 {Our vastly superior development is great compensation for the pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...d5 {2...d5 is also popular. Now e3 is a good developing move.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {The black knight is developed. Let's attack d5!} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black defends. We'll develop and put pressure on d5 once more.} 5.Nc3 5...c6 {It's possible for Black to go for this setup. In which case we should just develop our pieces...} 6.Nf3 6...Bd6 {And just with simple development, because of space and structure, White is better. We'll develop our last minor piece.} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black castles...} 8.O-O {And so do we. We can come back to trade bishops on f4, bring our rook to the more active c-file, and in general enjoy a better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...g6 {The move most masters play is 2...g6. Let's just develop aggressively with Nc3!} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {The bishop goes to g7. And here comes our h-pawn racing forward!} 4.h4 4...d5 {Black can react by putting a pawn in the center. Now e3 prepares the development of our light-squared bishop.} 5.e3 5...c6 {After adopting this light-square pawn structure... We'll play Nf3, and Black must cope with various positional threats that we have in this position.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {So if Black just continues with normal development...} 7.Bd3 {We can have an advantage just by continuing to develop. Our pieces are more active, we have a lead in development and we have more space.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...g6 {The move most masters play is 2...g6. Let's just develop aggressively with Nc3!} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {The bishop goes to g7. And here comes our h-pawn racing forward!} 4.h4 4...h6 {Attacking the bishop seems like a better try. We'll just go back one square.} 5.Bf4 5...Nf6 {The knight is developed.} 6.Qd3 {And now in a very unorthodox way, we can develop the queen. Black needs to be careful against our threat of Qg3, double attacking c7 and g6. Additionally, we have e4 ideas and 0-0-0.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...Nf6 {2...Nf6 is the most popular, but not the best. The reason for that is because we can damage the pawns. We'll just take. It seems the move 1...f5 here isn't beneficial for Black.} 3.Bxf6 3...exf6 {exf6 is the only move that doesn't lose. We'll begin putting our pawns on dark squares.} 4.e3 4...d5 {Black goes d5, controlling the center. But we'll immediately challenge Black's control by playing c4.} 5.c4 5...c6 {5...c6 is lousy. Even taking in the middle is good, because either Black will have an IQP in a bad structure or they'll lose central control. But quite honestly we could just continue building up pressure against that pawn with Nc3.} 6.Nc3 6...Bb4 {Black can get into a lot of trouble quickly... We can attack the bishop and add more pressure on d5.} 7.Qb3 7...Bxc3+ {If they take...} 8.bxc3 {Whose center is more impressive? At this point the engine is even recommending giving up material with 8...f4 because of how superior our structure is! Of course, we would just take that!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...Nf6 {2...Nf6 is the most popular, but not the best. The reason for that is because we can damage the pawns. We'll just take. It seems the move 1...f5 here isn't beneficial for Black.} 3.Bxf6 3...gxf6 {If this move is played, notice the weakness on h5...} 4.e4 {Black will lose castling rights. White has a winning position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Hopton Attack (2.Bg5) vs The Dutch / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/26e0f48b-6780-4128-85d6-6d0be6e7b3cd/chapters/80616b2e-be26-4dda-8b0d-4f2dc353fb99/studies/f573b42c-4c09-4b46-a6e1-342db824e781/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {And then our opponents go for the Dutch right away. We can still play Bg5 here! This is the Hopton Attack. The bishop can prove disruptive to Black.} 2.Bg5 2...h6 {2...h6 is very popular at the amateur level: the bishop is such a nuisance that Black wants to remove it immediately. I suggest we just stay on the diagonal that perturbs Black the most.} 3.Bh4 3...g5 {Tactically speaking, our bishop won't get trapped. I suggest we retreat to g3.} 4.Bg3 4...f4 {If Black plays f4...} 5.e3 {e3 attacks the f4 pawn and opens up the threat of checkmate on h5.} *